{"step_index":1016,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:17:09Z","thinking":"**Investigating Nginx Request Routing**\n\nDirect requests to the target IP and port succeed with a 200 OK status, but requests through Nginx fail with a 400 Bad Request. This indicates a discrepancy in how Nginx handles the requests. There's a strong suspicion Nginx is not forwarding the request to the expected destination. The question now is: to which endpoint is Nginx sending the traffic?\n\n\n**Examining Nginx Access Logs**\n\nInspecting Nginx access logs from the failing request reveals a 400 Bad Request response with a specific HTML payload size. This suggests Nginx is processing the request, but returning a client-side error. The log data is consistent, showing two attempts with the same outcome. The HTML payload content confirms the 400 status.\n\n\n**Identifying Upstream Request Differences**\n\nThe 400 response from Nginx confirms it's forwarding the request. The upstream is identified as Tomcat on a specific IP and port, returning a 400. Direct access to Tomcat from another host succeeds. To find the root cause, differences between the requests sent by Nginx and direct requests must be identified.\n\nCapturing the plaintext from Nginx and direct calls will be used to compare headers. Nginx uses SSL/TLS to connect upstream. Examining the Nginx configuration log format is the next logical step to capture upstream IP address and status codes. Alternatively, a method using a proxy or SSLKEYLOGFILE could allow inspection of the raw traffic.\n\n\n**Analyzing Nginx Request Headers**\n\nA python script will be run to capture the headers Nginx sends when proxying requests. This involves setting up a local HTTP server and sending requests through Nginx. Comparing the request Nginx sends with a direct curl request will highlight differences. The goal is to see if Nginx is modifying any headers.\n\nThe previous curl test is being re-examined to look for clues regarding the request Nginx is sending. Specifically, the request line and the HTTP version sent by Nginx will be checked. The question is: does the Nginx-proxied request change the client's HTTP/1.1 to another value?\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/home/koby/.ansible/kssh th2-revproxyp-fe1 \"echo '8&#JMl1Mwe0GyN' | sudo -S grep -rn 'AuditLog' /etc/nginx/modsec/ /etc/modsecurity/ 2>/dev/null || ls -la /var/log/modsec*\"","Cwd":"/home/koby","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking ModSecurity audit log path","toolSummary":"Find ModSecurity audit log"}}]}
