{"step_index":1039,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:18:20Z","thinking":"**Investigating Nginx's Proxy Behavior**\n\nDirect access to the target isn't required; the focus shifts to understanding Nginx's proxy failure. The core issue appears to be the 400 error when Nginx proxies to the target, contrasting with successful direct requests. An immediate comparison is needed between how Nginx forwards a request and how a direct command executes it. Exploring testing Nginx upstream configuration is now considered.\n\n\n**Analyzing Nginx Request Headers**\n\nConfirming we can capture traffic on the reverse proxy, now there is a need to examine the specific request headers sent by Nginx. The goal is to compare the headers sent by a failing Nginx proxy request to the successful direct request. Replicating the successful direct request, but directed through the Nginx proxy, becomes the immediate focus.\n\n\n**Testing Nginx Proxy Request Generation**\n\nAdding a test location allows direct inspection of the request Nginx forwards upstream. Configuring a local listener captures the complete raw HTTP request. Examining all location blocks within the configuration ensures the test is not blocked by a redirect.\n\nInvestigating `proxy_redirect` is crucial, however, the priority is to see the exact Nginx request first, before other possible configurations. Running a one-line script to capture the request data is now the next immediate step.\n\n\n**Comparing Request Headers & Ports**\n\nAn investigation of the failing request's headers is underway, starting by examining a successful direct curl command and observing that the `Host` header sent by `curl` is `172.30.198.50:9443` when using the same URL and port. The previous failed proxy request used `-H 'Host: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr'` instead. The discrepancy in the `Host` header is now suspect. The test location's proxy pass will be altered to reflect these findings.\n\nA local listening endpoint is configured to capture the requests. This will reveal the complete request Nginx is actually sending, allowing for direct comparison. Changing the production vhost is avoided.\n\n\n**Examining Direct Request Success**\n\nAnalyzing a direct request yields a successful 200 response with a specific URL, which highlights the discrepancy. Examining the headers of this successful call and comparing to the Nginx call is now crucial. The 400 error's source is questioned, considering if Nginx itself, rather than the backend, is generating the error.\n\nFurther investigation of the response headers returned by the Nginx 400 error is necessary to uncover the root cause. This confirms the 400 response originates from Nginx.\n\n\n**Identifying Tomcat's 400 Response**\n\nThe 400 error page isn't from Nginx; it's Tomcat's default, based on the `content-language` and HTML structure. The goal now is to determine why Tomcat returns 400 when accessed through Nginx but 200 directly. Examining Nginx's proxy configuration is the next step.\n\nAnalyzing the proxy configuration reveals a trailing slash in the `proxy_pass` directive, specifically `https://172.30.198.50:9443/`. A potential hypothesis is that this trailing slash causes the 400 error.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/home/koby/.ansible/kssh th2-revproxyp-fe1 \"curl -sS -i -k https://172.30.198.50:9443/centreon-map/api/latest/actuator/health -H 'Host: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr'\"","Cwd":"/home/koby","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Testing Host without port","toolSummary":"Curl Host without port directly to Tomcat"}}]}
